    ("Which rare celestial event occurs when a planet like Venus or Mercury crosses directly across the solar disk from Earth's view?", "Astronomical transit", ["Occultation", "Conjunction", "Opposition"], "18th-century expeditions timed transits of Venus to calculate the Astronomical Unit."),
    ("What astronomical alignment occurs when three celestial bodies—such as Sun, Earth, and Moon—line up in a straight line?", "Syzygy", ["Quadrature", "Aphelion", "Nadir"], "Produces solar eclipses, lunar eclipses, and spring tides."),
    ("What measurement in aviation and sailing represents vertical angle above the horizon, measured historically using a sextant?", "Altitude (Elevation angle)", ["Azimuth", "Declination", "Bearing"], "A marine sextant measures up to 120° using twin mirrors."),
    ("In cartography, which cylindrical map projection invented in 1569 preserves local angles and compass bearings while distorting polar areas?", "Mercator projection", ["Robinson projection", "Peters projection", "Winkel tripel"], "Invaluable for rhumb-line maritime navigation despite inflating Greenland relative to Africa."),
    ("Which equal-area pseudocylindrical map projection is commonly used for thematic density maps without area exaggeration?", "Mollweide projection", ["Mercator projection", "Gnomonic projection", "Stereographic projection"], "Maps the entire globe into a 2:1 ellipse."),
    ("Which typography classification lacks small projecting strokes at the ends of letterforms (like Helvetica or Inter)?", "Sans-serif", ["Serif", "Blackletter", "Slab serif"], "From French 'sans' (without) serif."),
    ("In typography, what vertical measure from baseline to baseline spaces lines of text?", "Leading", ["Kerning", "Tracking", "Ascender"], "Named after thin lead strips typesetters inserted between metal type rows."),
    ("In typography, what adjusts horizontal spacing specifically between an individual pair of adjacent letters (e.g., A and V)?", "Kerning", ["Tracking", "Leading", "Ligature"], "Tracking adjusts spacing uniformly across a whole word or block, whereas kerning tunes pairs."),
    ("In heraldry, what are the only two 'metals' traditionally paired with darker 'tinctures'?", "Gold (Or) and Silver (Argent)", ["Bronze and Copper", "Iron and Steel", "Gold and Platinum"], "Rule of tincture forbids placing metal upon metal or color upon color."),
    ("In international chess ratings, which Hungarian-American physics professor invented the modern statistical rating formula?", "Arpad Elo", ["Wilhelm Steinitz", "Mark Glickman", "Kenneth Harkness"], "Adopted by FIDE in 1970 and widely adapted into video game matchmaking."),
    ("In billiards and snooker, how many points is the black ball worth when potted legally?", "7 points", ["5 points", "6 points", "10 points"], "A maximum snooker break of 147 pots 15 reds with 15 blacks followed by all 6 colors."),
    ("In Japanese Sumo wrestling, what is the supreme grand champion rank that cannot be demoted once attained?", "Yokozuna", ["Ozeki", "Sekiwake", "Komusubi"], "Yokozuna are expected to retire if they can no longer uphold the rank's standard."),
    ("In Olympic fencing, which thrust-only weapon targets solely the opponent's torso and enforces right-of-way priority?", "Foil", ["Épée", "Sabre", "Kendo shinai"], "Unlike épée (entire body valid, no priority), foil restricts target area to the lamé-covered trunk."),
    ("Which vast temple complex in Siem Reap, Cambodia—originally dedicated to Vishnu under Suryavarman II—is Earth's largest religious monument?", "Angkor Wat", ["Borobudur", "Prambanan", "Bagan"], "Constructed in the early 12th century and gradually transformed into a Buddhist temple toward the century's end."),
    ("Which ancient Mesoamerican cacao-bean beverage was consumed unsweetened with chili and maize by Maya and Aztec elites?", "Xocolātl (Chocolate)", ["Pulque", "Chicha", "Yerba mate"], "Sugar was only added after cacao reached Europe in the 16th century."),
    ("Which rare blue-green pigment extracted in small quantities or synthesized first by ancient Egyptians was history's first synthetic pigment?", "Egyptian blue (Calcium copper silicate)", ["Prussian blue", "Cobalt blue", "Indigo"], "Produced around 2500 BC by heating silica, lime, and copper minerals."),
    ("Which bright blue synthetic pigment discovered accidentally in Berlin around 1706 replaced costly lapis lazuli in European skies?", "Prussian blue", ["Cerulean blue", "Phthalo blue", "Klein blue"], "Ferrocyanide pigment famously featured in Hokusai's 'Great Wave.'"),
    ("Which lustrous natural luxury protein fiber is harvested from Bombyx mori silkworm cocoons?", "Silk", ["Cashmere", "Vicuna", "Linen"], "Sericulture originated in Neolithic China over 5,000 years ago."),
    ("Which South American Andean camelid produces the rarest, finest legal luxury wool fiber on Earth?", "Vicuña", ["Alpaca", "Llama", "Guanaco"], "Protected wild vicuñas produce fibers roughly 12 microns thick—reserved for Inca royalty."),
    ("Which Italian violin-making city in Lombardy produced instruments by Antonio Stradivari and Giuseppe Guarneri?", "Cremona", ["Bologna", "Padua", "Parma"], "Cremona's traditional violin craftsmanship is inscribed by UNESCO."),
    ("Which ancient Roman concrete ingredient—volcanic ash from near Pozzuoli—enabled marine piers to harden underwater?", "Pozzolana", ["Quicklime", "Gypsum", "Slag"], "Creates stable aluminum-tobermorite crystals even in seawater."),
    ("Which ancient Roman civic official supervised public aqueducts and authored 'De Aquaeductu' in 97 AD?", "Sextus Julius Frontinus", ["Vitruvius", "Marcus Agrippa", "Pliny the Younger"], "Documented Rome's nine major aqueducts and anti-tampering water laws."),
    ("Which 1854 cholera investigation by Dr. John Snow in Soho, London mapped deaths around which public fixture?", "Broad Street water pump", ["Thames embankment", "Fleet sewer", "St. Giles well"], "Removing the pump handle halted the outbreak and founded modern spatial epidemiology."),
    ("Which 19th-century Hungarian obstetrician drastically cut childbed fever deaths by insisting doctors wash hands in chlorinated lime?", "Ignaz Semmelweis", ["Louis Pasteur", "Joseph Lister", "Robert Koch"], " tragically ridiculed by peers before germ theory validated his findings."),
    ("Which German microbiologist established the four postulates proving a specific microbe causes a specific disease?", "Robert Koch", ["Louis Pasteur", "Paul Ehrlich", "Ferdinand Cohn"], "Identified the causative bacilli of anthrax, tuberculosis, and cholera."),
    ("Which 19th-century British nurse and statistician pioneered polar-area ('coxcomb') charts to reform military hospitals?", "Florence Nightingale", ["Clara Barton", "Mary Seacole", "Edith Cavell"], "Became the first female Fellow of the Royal Statistical Society in 1859."),
    ("Which 18th-century English mathematician's posthumously published theorem updates prior probabilities given new evidence?", "Thomas Bayes", ["Pierre-Simon Laplace", "Blaise Pascal", "Abraham de Moivre"], "Bayesian inference now powers spam filters, medical diagnostics, and AI."),
    ("Which 1948 Bell Labs paper by Claude Shannon founded Information Theory and introduced the binary 'bit'?", "A Mathematical Theory of Communication", ["Computing Machinery and Intelligence", "On Computable Numbers", "Cybernetics"], "Established channel capacity and noiseless source coding limits."),
    ("Which 1948 book by Norbert Wiener coined the term for feedback control and communication in animals and machines?", "Cybernetics", ["Automata Studies", "The Mathematical Mind", "Principia Cybernetica"], "Derived from Greek 'kybernetes' (steersman)."),
    ("Which ancient Greek mechanical analog computer recovered in 1901 from a shipwreck predicted eclipses and Olympic cycles?", "Antikythera mechanism", ["Astrolabe of Hipparchus", "Archimedes' sphere", "Hero's aeolipile"], "Built around the 2nd–1st century BC with over 30 meshing bronze gears."),
    ("Which astronomical instrument used nested brass rings and a sighting alidade to compute star altitudes and prayer/sunrise times?", "Planispheric astrolabe", ["Sextant", "Armillary sphere", "Gnomon"], "Perfected in the medieval Islamic world and widely used by astronomers and navigators."),
    ("Which landmark 1759 offshore British lighthouse pioneered dovetailed interlocking granite blocks and hydraulic lime?", "Eddystone Lighthouse", ["Bell Rock Lighthouse", "Cordouan Lighthouse", "Pharos of Alexandria"], "Designed by John Smeaton, who also coined the term 'civil engineer' and rediscovered hydraulic lime."),
    ("Which Scottish engineer developed the separate condenser in 1769, quadrupling the efficiency of Newcomen steam engines?", "James Watt", ["Richard Trevithick", "George Stephenson", "Thomas Savery"], "Partnered with Matthew Boulton in Birmingham, giving his name to the SI unit of power."),
    ("Which British locomotive built by Robert and George Stephenson won the 1829 Rainhill Trials, setting the template for steam railways?", "Stephenson's Rocket", ["Puffing Billy", "Mallard", "Flying Scotsman"], "Combined a multi-tubular boiler with a blastpipe exhaust chimney."),
    ("Which international distress Morse code signal (... --- ...) was adopted in 1906 not as an acronym, but for its unmistakable continuous rhythm?", "SOS", ["CQD", "MAYDAY", "PAN"], "Chosen at the 1906 International Radiotelegraph Convention in Berlin."),
